```java
Map session = new HashMap();
session.put("user", new User("Alice"));

// Some caller accidentally adds a wrong type:
session.put("user", 42);

// Much later, in unrelated code:
User u = (User) session.get("user");
// throws ClassCastException at runtime — 
// debugging finds the bug 30 files away from the line that threw
```

```java
Map<String, User> session = new HashMap<>();
session.put("user", new User("Alice"));

// Any later code trying to put the wrong type:
session.put("user", 42); // COMPILE ERROR — int is not a User

User u = session.get("user"); // no cast needed
```
The bug can't even reach runtime. The compiler's type check pays for itself the first time this happens in a real codebase.

Wildcards (`? extends T` and `? super T`) are the generic feature that matters most for API design, and the one most developers learn last. The rule is captured by **PECS: Producer Extends, Consumer Super**.

When a method *reads* from a collection (the collection produces values), use `? extends T`. This lets the caller pass `List<Integer>`, `List<Double>`, or any subtype of `Number`.

```java
public double sum(List<? extends Number> numbers) {
    double total = 0;
    for (Number n : numbers) total += n.doubleValue();
    return total;
}
```

When a method *writes* to a collection (the collection consumes values), use `? super T`. This lets the caller pass `List<Number>`, `List<Object>`, or any supertype.

```java
public void addIntegers(List<? super Integer> target) {
    target.add(1);
    target.add(2);
}
```

Without wildcards, `sum(List<Integer>)` would not accept a `List<Integer>` if the parameter was typed as `List<Number>` — because `List<Integer>` is *not* a subtype of `List<Number>` (this is called **invariance**, and it's a direct consequence of erasure and type safety). Wildcards restore the flexibility that erasure takes away.

When you want to accept a collection that **produces values** for you to read, use `? extends T`.

```java
public double sum(List<? extends Number> list) {
    double total = 0;
    for (Number n : list) {        // safe read as Number
        total += n.doubleValue();
    }
    return total;
}

List<Integer> ints = List.of(1, 2, 3);
List<Double> doubles = List.of(1.5, 2.5);
sum(ints);     // OK — List<Integer> matches ? extends Number
sum(doubles);  // OK — List<Double> matches too
```

**Why you can't add**:
```java
void bad(List<? extends Number> list) {
    list.add(1);   // COMPILE ERROR
    list.add(1.5); // COMPILE ERROR
    list.add(null); // allowed — null is assignable to any type
}
```
The compiler doesn't know if the actual list is `List<Integer>` or `List<Double>` — adding an `Integer` to a `List<Double>` would be unsafe. So it refuses all adds except `null`.

When you want to accept a collection that **consumes values** you provide, use `? super T`.

```java
public void addOneToTen(List<? super Integer> list) {
    for (int i = 1; i <= 10; i++) {
        list.add(i);   // safe — the list holds Integer or a supertype
    }
}

List<Integer> ints = new ArrayList<>();
List<Number>  nums = new ArrayList<>();
List<Object>  objs = new ArrayList<>();
addOneToTen(ints); // OK
addOneToTen(nums); // OK — Integer fits into List<Number>
addOneToTen(objs); // OK — Integer fits into List<Object>
```

**Reads give you `Object` only**:
```java
void read(List<? super Integer> list) {
    Object o = list.get(0);    // OK — everything extends Object
    Integer i = list.get(0);   // COMPILE ERROR — could be Number or Object
}
```
The list might contain `Number` or `Object`; reading as `Integer` would be unsafe.

The JDK itself models the PECS rule in its signature for `Collections.copy`:

```java
public static <T> void copy(List<? super T> dest, List<? extends T> src) {
    for (int i = 0; i < src.size(); i++) {
        dest.set(i, src.get(i));
    }
}
```

- `src` is a **producer** of `T` — we read from it — so `? extends T`.
- `dest` is a **consumer** of `T` — we write to it — so `? super T`.

This lets you call `copy(List<Number>, List<Integer>)` — source is `Integer` (a subtype of `T=Number`), destination is `Number` (a supertype). Without both wildcards, this call wouldn't compile.

Use `?` (unbounded) when your method doesn't care about the element type — it just works with the collection structure.

```java
public void printAll(List<?> list) {
    for (Object o : list) {
        System.out.println(o);
    }
}

public int countElements(Collection<?> c) {
    return c.size();
}
```

**Why not use raw `List`?** `List<?>` is safer. A raw `List` lets you add anything without warnings; `List<?>` refuses to add anything except `null`, which forces you to treat it as read-only. The unchecked-warning discipline catches bugs that raw types silently accept.

**Don't use `?` when you actually need to relate two parameters**: if you're writing `<T> void transform(List<T> src, List<T> dst)`, use a named type parameter, not wildcards — wildcards would lose the relationship between the two lists.

**Producer — use `? extends T`** (the parameter produces values your method reads).

```java
// We're READING elements out of the list
public double sum(List<? extends Number> nums) {
    double total = 0;
    for (Number n : nums) total += n.doubleValue();
    return total;
}
sum(List.of(1, 2, 3));           // Integer list OK
sum(List.of(1.5, 2.5));          // Double list OK
```

**Consumer — use `? super T`** (the parameter consumes values your method produces).

```java
// We're WRITING Integers INTO the list
public void fillWithIntegers(List<? super Integer> target) {
    for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) target.add(i);
}
fillWithIntegers(new ArrayList<Integer>()); // OK
fillWithIntegers(new ArrayList<Number>());  // OK
fillWithIntegers(new ArrayList<Object>());  // OK
```

**Both — use a named type parameter `<T>`** (the parameter does both read and write of T).

```java
// We both READ and WRITE T — wildcards can't express that T is the same
public <T> void swap(List<T> list, int i, int j) {
    T temp = list.get(i);   // read T
    list.set(i, list.get(j)); // write T
    list.set(j, temp);        // write T
}
```

The JDK's own copy method is the textbook PECS example:

```java
public static <T> void copy(List<? super T> dest, List<? extends T> src) {
    for (int i = 0; i < src.size(); i++) {
        dest.set(i, src.get(i));
    }
}
```

**Why both wildcards?**
- `src` is a **producer of T** — we only read from it — so `? extends T` lets callers pass any subtype.
- `dest` is a **consumer of T** — we only write to it — so `? super T` lets callers pass any supertype.

**Flexibility this unlocks:**
```java
List<Object> dest  = new ArrayList<>(Arrays.asList(null, null, null));
List<Integer> src  = List.of(1, 2, 3);
Collections.copy(dest, src);   // Integer is subtype of T, Object is supertype
```
Without PECS, this call wouldn't compile — `List<Integer>` is not a `List<Object>` due to invariance. PECS unlocks the flexibility without sacrificing type safety.

**When a parameter both reads and writes T**: wildcards break down. `void transform(List<? extends T>)` can't call `list.add(...)`; `List<? super T>` can't cleanly expose `T get(int)`. Use a named type parameter instead.

**Don't wildcard return types**: `List<? extends Number> getNumbers()` forces every caller to use wildcard types throughout their code. Return `List<T>` or a named subtype. PECS applies to parameters, not return values.

**Wildcards don't help when two parameters must share the same type**:
```java
// WRONG — loses the relationship
void transfer(List<? extends T> src, List<? super T> dst) { ... }
// BETTER when you need a bound, sometimes:
<T> void transfer(List<T> src, List<? super T> dst) { ... }
```

**The 'get and put' rule** is the operational form of PECS: if your method only **gets** from the parameter, use `extends`. If it only **puts** into the parameter, use `super`. If it does both with the same type, use a named type parameter.

**Don't fall back to raw types**. `List` is always worse than `List<?>` — the raw type silently lets you break type safety; the wildcard form refuses unsafe operations.

**Workaround 1 — Object array + unchecked cast (common in JDK code):**
```java
public class Stack<T> {
    private T[] elements;

    @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
    public Stack(int capacity) {
        elements = (T[]) new Object[capacity]; // runtime is Object[]; compiler warning suppressed
    }
}
```
You're lying to the compiler — the runtime array is `Object[]`, not `T[]`. This is safe **only if you never leak the array outside the class** (never return it, never let callers access it directly). The JDK's `ArrayList` works exactly this way. Callers interact through `get(int)`, which casts on the way out.

**Workaround 2 — `Array.newInstance` with a `Class<T>` token:**
```java
public static <T> T[] newArray(Class<T> clazz, int size) {
    @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
    T[] array = (T[]) Array.newInstance(clazz, size);
    return array;
}

String[] arr = newArray(String.class, 10); // runtime: actual String[]
```
This creates a true `String[]` at runtime because you passed the `Class` token. The array is reifiable — safe to return, safe to pass around, will throw `ArrayStoreException` on wrong assignment.

**Workaround 3 — just use `List<T>` instead.** In modern Java, `ArrayList<T>` is the idiomatic choice; generic arrays are mostly a library-author problem, not an application-code one.
